Benchmark suite for REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW ... WHERE ...
==========================================================

    ./run.sh --help
    ./run.sh --label before
    ...make a change, rebuild...
    ./run.sh --label after

Eight workloads, chosen from USE-CASES.md so that each isolates a DIFFERENT
cost driver rather than a different business domain.  Adding a ninth is only
worth it if it isolates something none of these do.

  projection   the cheapest possible shape; isolates fixed cost
  aggregate    GROUP BY, predicate on the grouping key
  join_agg     join + GROUP BY -- the shape the on-list benchmark used, so this
               is the comparability anchor against the numbers Adam posted
  window       rank() OVER (PARTITION BY): push-down through WindowAgg, and
               blast radius forces the scope to a whole partition
  expensive    join + to_tsvector + GIN: per-row cost and index maintenance
               dominate, so the ~500-row crossover does not transfer here
  nonkey       predicate on an indexed NON-key column -- the shape whose
               disjoint scopes are supposed to refresh in parallel
  timerange    range predicate over buckets: large scope, bare form
  recursive    the predicate may not push into the recursive term at all

Sweep axes: --scales --groups --spans --forms --shapes --clients --overlap
--mutate --sync.  Give any of them a comma-separated list.

Every result is normalised against a full rebuild of the same matview, so runs
from different machines or builds remain comparable to each other:

  us_per_scope_row  latency / rows the predicate actually selected
  vs_full_per_row   how many times a full rebuild's per-row cost that is

The last column is the one to watch.  A partial refresh pays while
vs_full_per_row * scope_rows < mv_rows; report.sql prints that verdict.

Four things that will otherwise bite you
----------------------------------------

0. `make clean` AFTER EVERY `configure`.  This tree is configured without
   --enable-depend, so `autodepend` is empty in src/Makefile.global and no
   .deps files are generated.  make therefore has no idea that any .o depends
   on pg_config.h: change a configure flag, rebuild, and it relinks the stale
   objects and reports success.  The binary is new, its contents are not.

   This has produced two silently wrong builds here -- an "-O2" build that was
   still -O0 with assertions on and benchmarked identically to the debug one,
   and an "--enable-injection-points" build whose server answered "injection
   points are not supported by this build".  Both said "configured / built /
   installed".

   Verify, do not assume.  `SHOW debug_assertions` for cassert, and
   `find src/backend -name '*.o' -newer src/include/pg_config.h | wc -l`
   should be ~893 after a configure change, not 1.  Or configure the tree
   with --enable-depend once and stop having to think about it.

1. RECORD THE BUILD.  bench_result stores pg_version and debug_assertions and
   run.sh warns when assertions are on.  A -O0 --enable-cassert number is not
   comparable with a -O2 one -- not even as a ratio.  Both were tried here and
   several ratios moved by more than 2x.

2. pgbench COMMITS ONCE PER REFRESH.  Measurements inside a single transaction
   (a trigger-driven refresh) and measurements with a commit each (a drain
   process) are different workloads and differ by an order of magnitude here.
   --sync off removes the WAL flush but not the commit itself.  Decide which
   one you are asking about before reading the number.

3. THE FULL-REFRESH BASELINE IS TAKEN RIGHT AFTER A BULK LOAD, so it can be
   contaminated by the setup's dirty buffers -- it has been seen to vary 2.6x
   between runs of the same configuration.  If baselines look unstable, insert
   a CHECKPOINT and a settle before bench_baseline(), or pin one baseline and
   reuse it across runs.

4. START IT WITH detach.sh, NOT WITH `&`.  A sweep runs for tens of minutes,
   which is longer than the shell that starts it is likely to live.  Three
   sweeps were lost here before the cause was looked at rather than guessed at,
   and the guesses -- out of memory, out of disk -- were both wrong: memory
   peaked well under the limit, the filesystem was at 27%, and the postmaster
   went on running throughout.  What died each time was the process tree of the
   shell, and the run was in it.

   detach.sh puts the run in its own session, so it is no longer reachable by a
   signal aimed at the caller's process group.  This is what pg_ctl does for the
   postmaster, and is why the postmaster survived while the benchmark did not.

     ./detach.sh /tmp/bench.log --forms spi,querytree --label p21-O2

   A sweep that dies anyway loses only the combinations it had not reached:
   run.sh inserts into bench_result one row at a time rather than at the end,
   and caches the full-rebuild baseline per (workload, scale, groups), so a
   restart neither loses finished work nor re-pays for the baselines.

   Do not run anything else on the box while a sweep is going.  Best-of-N
   absorbs a brief disturbance, but an initdb or a compile in parallel with a
   measurement is not brief.

Files
-----
  workloads.sql  the eight workloads, as data
  harness.sql    bench_result, bench_setup(), bench_baseline(), bench_scope_rows()
  run.sh         the driver; generates pgbench scripts and records results
  detach.sh      starts run.sh in its own session so it outlives the caller
  report.sql     normalised cost, concurrency scaling, and a rebuild verdict
